{
  "teamId": 146,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Miami Marlins opened the year with genuine promise. Jazz Chisholm Jr. announced his presence with a go-ahead home run on Opening Weekend, and the club raced out to a 12-8 April record that had Fish fans daring to dream. The bullpen showed cracks even in those early weeks — Anthony Bender surrendered a gut-punch homer on the very same night Chisholm electrified the crowd — but the wins kept coming and the mood stayed optimistic.\n\nMay shattered that optimism almost immediately. The Marlins went a brutal 7-19, their worst month of the season, undone by a bullpen that simply could not hold leads. Cole Sulser surrendered a three-run walk-off shot to former Marlin Jorge Alfaro. Anthony Bender allowed a go-ahead blast to Pavin Smith. The offense, posting an anemic .657 OPS on the year, could rarely cushion the blow. By the end of May, Miami had already squandered most of the goodwill built in April, and the playoff dream felt distant.\n\nJune brought a surprising resurrection. A 15-13 record, a Jerar Encarnacion grand slam, a walk-off from Willians Astudillo, and back-to-back late-game heroics from Avisaíl García and Sandy Alcantara gave the club a pulse heading into the summer. July was similarly competitive at 13-15, filled with high-drama moments swinging in both directions — Jesús Sánchez launching a two-run blast on Independence Day weekend, Bryan De La Cruz providing pop, but Tanner Scott repeatedly entering games only to watch rallies crumble via extra-base hits and a brutal throwing error.\n\nAugust was the death knell, a catastrophic 8-20 collapse that buried any lingering wild-card hope. September and October produced flashes of pride — Avisaíl García's grand slam in late September, Bryan De La Cruz's clutch single to open October — but they were consolation prizes for a team that finished 69-93, outscored by 90 runs, and watching the playoffs from home. The Marlins' story in 2022 was one of a pitching staff good enough to compete (a 3.87 ERA is respectable) harnessed to an offense that could not sustain pressure, and a bullpen that turned winnable games into losses at the worst possible moments.",
